> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Windows 10 1607+ / 11 x64 only.** Uses Win32 GUI, Job Object, WinEventHook, low-level input hooks, per-monitor DPI awareness v2, and a local Microsoft Edge install.
>
> You do **not** need to "Install app / PWA" in Edge first. This version launches `msedge.exe --app=<tokenUrl>` directly.
>
> "App window" here means Chromium's app-mode window, which has nothing to do with an installed PWA (that was the v1 approach).

---

## What it does

From dsh 0.1.2 the web root is authenticated: each process prints a one-shot URL with `?token=`. The browser exchanges that token for a session cookie, then lands on a clean root. Opening `http://127.0.0.1:3080/` with no token returns 401.

This repo folds that flow into a console-less exe:

1. Single-instance check: if an instance is already running, just bring its window to the front and exit.
2. Show a native splash immediately (cancellable).
3. Launch `dsh web --no-open` through pnpm's `node.exe`, stdout on a pipe.
4. Parse `dsh web: http://127.0.0.1:<port>/?token=...`.
5. Open Edge `--app=<tokenUrl>` once, maximize it, then publish a tray icon.
6. Closing the window (X / Alt+F4 / Ctrl+Shift+W) only **hides it to the tray**; the backend keeps running.
7. Tray menu → Quit → Job Object kills the node tree and frees the port.

---

## Close semantics

| Action | Result |
| :--- | :--- |
| Left-click the title-bar X | Hide to tray, backend keeps running |
| `Alt+F4` (app window focused) | Same |
| `Ctrl+Shift+W` (app window focused) | Same |
| Left-click the tray icon / run the exe again | Bring the existing window to the front |
| Right-click tray icon → Show window | Same |
| Right-click tray icon → Quit and stop backend | Close window + kill process tree + remove tray icon + exit |
| Window destroyed by anything else (taskbar preview close, browser crash, …) | Reclaim the backend and exit too — no zombie processes |

None of the three "hide to tray" paths shows any notification, and none of them stops the backend.

Hotkeys are intercepted only while the **app window is focused**, so they never steal `Alt+F4` / `Ctrl+Shift+W` from other programs.

---

## Quick start

### 1. Prerequisites

- Windows 10 1607+ / 11 x64 with Microsoft Edge
- Global dsh **≥ 0.1.2**: `pnpm add -g @deepseek-ai/dsh`

No Edge PWA install required.

### 2. Create shortcuts

Download the [latest release zip](https://github.com/2bitbit/dsh-desktop/releases/latest), extract it, then in PowerShell:

```powershell
# Interactive: folder picker
.\install_shortcut.ps1

# Explicit workspace
.\install_shortcut.ps1 -TargetDirectory "D:\Your\Workspace"
```

Shortcuts land on both **Desktop and Start Menu**. Clicking one is equivalent to running `dsh web --no-open` in that directory, then opening a maximized Edge app window with the process token.

The tray icon comes from the exe's embedded resource (`src/tray_icon.ico`); the shortcut icon uses the same one.

---

## Lifecycle

```
Shortcut (--cwd <dir>)
        │
        ▼
dsh_pwa_shim.exe ── single-instance check (already running → focus it, exit)
        │
        ├─ splash
        │
        ├─ Job Object ──▶ node (dsh web --no-open)
        │                     │
        │                     └─ stdout: dsh web: http://127.0.0.1:<port>/?token=***
        │
        ├─ Edge --app=<tokenUrl>  (maximized)
        │
        ├─ tray icon published
        │
        ├─ X / Alt+F4 / Ctrl+Shift+W ──▶ hide to tray (backend keeps running)
        │
        └─ tray menu "Quit" ──▶ TerminateJobObject ──▶ reclaim process tree / free port
```

While running it sleeps on `SetWinEventHook` + `MsgWaitForMultipleObjects` — **zero polling, 0% CPU**.

How the X gets intercepted: the shim installs a `WH_MOUSE_LL` low-level mouse hook. On left-button-down inside the window's top band it asks the app window for `WM_NCHITTEST`; Chromium answers truthfully for its self-drawn title bar, returning `HTCLOSE` for the close button. Only a `HTCLOSE` hit swallows the click and hides the window — normal clicks inside the page are untouched.

---

## CLI

| Option | Description | Example |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `--cwd <path>` / `-w <path>` | dsh working directory | `dsh_pwa_shim.exe --cwd "D:\Projects\Demo"` |
| `--debug` / `-d` | debug log + error dialogs | `dsh_pwa_shim.exe --debug` |

Two log files next to the exe (truncated on each launch, appended while running, size-capped; tokens redacted):

| File | Contents | Cap |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `dsh_pwa_shim.log` | shim log | 512 KB |
| `dsh_web_console.log` | `dsh web` stdout/stderr | 2 MB |

---

## Changes since v1.0

- **No preinstalled Edge PWA**, no hardcoded `--app-id`.
- **No TCP probe on port 3080.** Parses the authenticated URL this process printed (port may vary).
- Native splash: the browser does not open until the token exists, so no 401 flash.
- Captured window is maximized.
- `dsh web` console output is written to `dsh_web_console.log`.
- Tray residency + close semantics (X / Alt+F4 / Ctrl+Shift+W hide to tray; the tray menu really quits).
- Single instance: running the exe again only focuses the existing window.
- The tray icon is embedded in the exe; no external icon file needed.

---

## Build from source

Needs one of MinGW-w64 / LLVM Clang / MSVC, plus the matching resource compiler (`windres` or `rc.exe`):

```powershell
.\scripts\build.ps1
```

The script first compiles `src/dsh_pwa_shim.rc` (which embeds the tray icon `src/tray_icon.ico`) into a resource object, then links it together with `src/dsh_pwa_shim.cpp`.

Output: `bin/dsh_pwa_shim.exe`. The GCC path links with `-static -static-libgcc -static-libstdc++`, so the binary does not depend on MinGW runtime DLLs.

> The GCC path is tested on MinGW-w64 GCC 15; the Clang and MSVC paths are untested.

